API route cache and worker startup
=================================

Static trailing-slash aliases are built from the registered FastAPI route
metadata. Ordinary requests no longer generate the complete OpenAPI document
just to discover static paths. Documentation remains available and is generated
when requested. Nested router prefixes and include_in_schema visibility retain
the previous alias boundary. Dynamic paths retain their registered spelling.

Installing or removing plugin routes must call invalidate_route_cache(). The
next request rebuilds the static path inventory and clears the memoized OpenAPI
document. This does not authorize requests or bypass endpoint dependencies.

The pinned FastAPI version retains included routers with prefix and visibility
contexts. The inventory walker relies on that representation. On upgrades, run
tests/core/test_route_cache_inventory.py and tests/plugins/test_plugin_install_e2e.py;
the full-application inventory comparison must agree with the OpenAPI static
path set. An upstream route representation change requires review, not a blanket
fallback to generating schemas on every worker's first request.

This removes one measured source of cold-request work. It does not establish a
latency SLA, solve process-local camera stream ownership, change Gunicorn worker
counts or enable preload. Check packaged worker recycling under real load before
making availability or capacity claims. Customer hardware, database recovery and
multi-host failover require their separate release qualifications.
